# This workspace is a git repository, and every mutation the Corpus server makes
# is auto-committed with the acting party as git author. `git log` is therefore
# the audit trail of the corpus itself. What follows is everything that must stay
# out of it.

# Runtime state. Everything under .corpus/ is secret (config.json holds the
# bearer token), derived (cache.db rebuilds from the files), or transient
# (server.pid, server.log, jobs/, attachments/, seen.json, HALT).
.corpus/*

# ...except the install manifest, which is none of those three. It records which
# template files this workspace was installed with, and at which tool version —
# provenance, not runtime state. Tracking it means a clone carries its own
# upgrade baseline instead of `corpus workspace upgrade` having to guess which
# files were edited and which are simply old.
!.corpus/template-manifest.json

# ...and except the queue directory skeleton. The server moves event files between
# one directory per queue status — `corpus init` creates them all from the status
# list itself, so counting them here would only go stale — and a clone of this
# workspace has to arrive with them already present. The event JSON files
# themselves are runtime state and stay ignored; each directory's .gitkeep is
# what git actually tracks.
!.corpus/queue/
.corpus/queue/*/*.json

# OS clutter that would otherwise be auto-committed alongside real edits.
.DS_Store
